WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.680
Hey folks, welcome to the cute woodland creatures&nbsp;
edition of my precon reviews. A plurality of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.640
patrons thought I should look at the group hug&nbsp;
precon, and in this video I’m going to accomplish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.840
a simple set of tasks—I’m going to lay out what&nbsp;
I think the deck is trying to do, I’m going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.880
describe what I like about the deck, and finally&nbsp;
I’m going to talk about what I dislike about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.160
and what changes I’d make to fix the things I&nbsp;
dislike. With that in mind, let’s jump right in.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.080
The commander for this deck is the honorable Ms.&nbsp;
Bumbleflower, and the first thing I note here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:36.120
that her effect is quite cool and unique.&nbsp;
Just the line “Whenever you cast a spell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.640
target opponent draws a card” is wild—part of&nbsp;
what lets me consider this a group hug commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.520
of sorts is the sheer amount of value you can&nbsp;
give to opponents. There are general politics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.200
commanders like Breena and 2023 Rocco that&nbsp;
passively offer opponents resources but have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:58.640
a built-in way to twist things back in your favor,&nbsp;
but with Ms. Bumbleflower you’re actively handing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.800
your opponents cards when you cast spells, and&nbsp;
if you don’t cast exactly two spells per turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.800
she gives your opponents more cards than she gives&nbsp;
to you. The second spell clause gives you cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.840
when the ability triggers for the second time each&nbsp;
turn, and this really hones in the blue identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.800
of this card—you want to cast two spells per turn&nbsp;
unless you just love giving your opponents cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.400
In the decklist, we can see a number of things&nbsp;
that fit with what this commander is doing—for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.640
one, there are a fair number of decently chunky&nbsp;
beaters, most of which would enjoy being given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:35.600
+1/+1 counters and flying. A lot of these utilize&nbsp;
+1/+1 counters in some way, and to go with this we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:41.160
can see a handful of other cards with some sort&nbsp;
of counter synergies. In addition to the +1/+1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.880
counter beats, there is some degree of giving&nbsp;
other players resources present, as you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:52.440
expect for a deck labeled “group hug”. There are 5&nbsp;
cards that utilize Bloomburrow’s “gift” mechanic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.120
where a spell can have an additional effect if an&nbsp;
opponent is offered some sort of resource. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:03.920
are also 11 other cards I’d describe as huggy&nbsp;
and 6 cards that are vaguely political in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:10.360
capacity, though the line between these categories&nbsp;
I’ve constructed is a bit fuzzy. There are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:15.160
other cards whose effects depend on the actions&nbsp;
of your opponents and you could argue that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:20.040
are political, though this deck is relatively&nbsp;
beatdown oriented so I’m not convinced it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:25.520
be easy to talk opponents into feeding these.&nbsp;
Results may vary, basically. It seems like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.280
deck is looking to gradually develop beaters and&nbsp;
value engines while currying favor with various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:35.960
opponents, right up until it starts to snowball&nbsp;
out of control and can drop the friendly pretexts.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:40.800
So, that’s the deck. But what do I think about&nbsp;
what this deck is doing? Well, I’ll start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.120
what I like, and there’s actually quite a lot of&nbsp;
that. This deck broadly has a decent card quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:51.040
a focused enough plan to win, and good amounts&nbsp;
of different types of support cards. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.840
several quite-decent-quality beaters, and&nbsp;
Ms. Bumbleflower is solid for purposes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.440
amplifying and pushing through damage for both&nbsp;
these and the cards that are more in the realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:05.760
of being support creatures. Side note—I find the&nbsp;
aesthetic contrast between Psychosis Crawler and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:11.360
the rest of this deck very funny. The +1/+1&nbsp;
counter package of the deck, though not super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:16.200
wide, has some real all-stars in it—there are&nbsp;
several different threats that grow over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:22.280
and among the handful of counter synergy pieces,&nbsp;
Rishkar Peema Renegade is a pretty sweet ramp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:28.320
tool and Kalonian Hydra is counter-pumping bomb&nbsp;
that will run away with games if left unchecked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.160
The deck will likely be able to politically&nbsp;
navigate through the game using its commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.600
offering some opponents resources while beating&nbsp;
down others with its suite of growing threats.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.840
As I mentioned earlier, the quantities of support&nbsp;
cards here are also quite decent. There are 14&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:48.080
cards I’d categorize as ramp and 15 cards I’d&nbsp;
categorize as draw, and these should prepare the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.280
deck decently well to accelerate into a gameplan&nbsp;
and keep up momentum even if the deck hits some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.880
setbacks. I playtested this deck a number of&nbsp;
times, and the average curve out that this deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.760
manages is far better than in either of the other&nbsp;
precons I’ve looked at—the deck is able to ramp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.040
build resources with the commander, and set&nbsp;
up value engines with quite a decent degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.960
of consistency. Obviously when I’m playtesting the&nbsp;
deck I’m kind of abstracting away the cards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.440
opponents are being given, and I like to think&nbsp;
this is reasonable enough. A lot of EDH games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:23.560
will have one or two players who are lagging a bit&nbsp;
behind for one reason or another, and while giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:29.040
these players resources doesn’t necessarily come&nbsp;
at no cost, the gameplay cost will be lower than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.960
the cost of giving all opponents resources.&nbsp;
For that reason I mostly think about this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:39.520
resource donation as being probably not an issue&nbsp;
as long as this deck is meaningfully progressing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.880
its own game plan at the same time. While you&nbsp;
could reasonably argue that this means the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:48.240
should be held to a slightly higher bar than&nbsp;
some other precons in terms of how it plays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.360
it most likely passes even that bar as well—this&nbsp;
is just a deck that plays pretty smoothly. A big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.680
part of this is likely the manabase—the manabase&nbsp;
of this deck is pretty similar to the manabase of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.720
the Outlaws at Thunder Junction precon I looked&nbsp;
at a few months back, which is to say, it has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.720
healthy land count and a really robust amount of&nbsp;
color-fixing that results in quite healthy symbol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:17.520
counts. Now, this isn’t at no cost—of the 38&nbsp;
lands, 9 always enter tapped and 7 sometimes do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:23.560
and I’d guess those two figures average out to&nbsp;
roughly a one-third tapland quantity. While this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:28.760
is a bit higher than most players will want long&nbsp;
term, it’s far from a terrible starting point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:34.440
and this tapland density doesn’t feel particularly&nbsp;
bad in practice. In addition, in the lens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:39.000
considering this precon as a product, giving&nbsp;
players more fixing lands to work with is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:44.360
undoubtedly a good thing, even if it means the&nbsp;
deck includes taplands that might later get cut.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.920
Finally, this deck has an adequate amount of&nbsp;
ways to interact with things opponents are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:53.320
doing. This deck has two counterspells, three&nbsp;
board wipes, five single target removal spells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:58.760
and a smattering of other cards that play other&nbsp;
related purposes such as slowing opponents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:04.160
defending against major attacks, and protecting&nbsp;
key pieces. Now, one of those categories I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:09.560
been historically less of a fan of—I find that&nbsp;
space dedicated to defensive spells would often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:14.680
be better allocated on actual removal or on&nbsp;
things that push a player’s own gameplan. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:19.600
I will be cutting a couple defensive cards&nbsp;
from this deck—kind of. But this deck’s plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:25.120
of building up large, evasive threats means that&nbsp;
life total races won’t be an uncommon situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:30.240
and having a fog around in that case might&nbsp;
be quite helpful. The deck’s political tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:34.360
namely the commander, also give it a decent&nbsp;
option for negotiating with opponents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:39.000
and avoiding pressure if it seems worth the&nbsp;
price of an opponent having some cards. I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.520
see the appeal of this deck’s political and&nbsp;
defensive elements, and I imagine they work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:48.520
decently in tandem with the deck’s more concrete&nbsp;
removal for keeping the game under control.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:53.240
Alright, it’s time to get into what I dislike&nbsp;
about this precon, and also what I’d change to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:58.360
help it out with those things. I mentioned that&nbsp;
the manabase of the deck reminds me of the OTJ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:04.040
Gonti precon, and there’s one other way that this&nbsp;
precon reminds me of that one. In the video where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:08.800
I reviewed the Gonti deck, I highlighted that&nbsp;
it feels like the decklist was designed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:14.800
a generic theft deck rather than a semi-theft&nbsp;
deck tailored to what Gonti does. This precon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.520
is similar in that it seems like Wizards of&nbsp;
the Coast added a bunch of group hug stuff to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:24.440
this precon because the commander does something&nbsp;
vaguely group hug-y, but Ms. Bumbleflower is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:30.400
a generic group hug commander. No, Snail’s theory&nbsp;
of optimal Bumbleflower dictates that I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.120
be sharing card draw with my weakest opponent,&nbsp;
maybe playing politics with it here and there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:40.120
and then also making optimal usage of the +1/+1&nbsp;
counters and flying that Ms. Bumbleflower gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:45.200
me. In addition, a lot of the group hug elements&nbsp;
currently in the deck don’t even feel like they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:49.480
play that nicely with the rest of what the&nbsp;
deck is doing. Basically, what I dislike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:54.280
about this deck is that it's a bit unfocused,&nbsp;
including lots of stuff that is outside what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:59.440
believe the deck wants to be—a high velocity,&nbsp;
politics-heavy +1/+1 counter beatdown deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:05.200
Now, I understand if somebody wants to play&nbsp;
harder into the group hug elements, since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:10.680
group-hug-ish precons don’t come often, but in&nbsp;
my mind there’s just not enough to work with here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:15.920
for that. I will preserve the group hug element&nbsp;
on some level, though, in that I don’t feel the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:20.840
need to prevent or take advantage of the cards&nbsp;
that Ms. Bumbleflower gives my opponents—they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:25.320
allowed to have a little gift. Another thing&nbsp;
that might be nice is making the deck slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:29.440
lower-curved to allow for double-spelling&nbsp;
with Ms. Bumbleflower earlier in the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:36.200
as the current deck is a bit high curved for my&nbsp;
taste. Overall, I’m going to make 20 card swaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:40.120
and all of these will be done with a&nbsp;
precon budget in mind, so I’m not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:45.920
to add anything over $3 and I’m going to try to&nbsp;
end up at a lower overall price for the deck.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:49.840
With all that in mind, I’m going to start by&nbsp;
cutting some of the huggier elements of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.760
deck. I like the idea of extra land plays in this&nbsp;
deck, but I don’t really want my opponents to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.760 --> 00:09:00.000
have extra land plays if they’re gonna be getting&nbsp;
fed cards by me, so I choose to swap out Rites of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.360
Flourishing for Wayward Swordtooth and Ghirapur&nbsp;
Orrery for Case of the Locked Hothouse. Case is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:11.520
a really powerful card in general, and Swordtooth&nbsp;
is a nice option as a creature to give flying once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:17.680
Ms. Bumbleflower is in play. I’m also choosing to&nbsp;
cut Tenuous Truce and Intellectual Offering—these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.760
cards are doing something similar to what the&nbsp;
commander is doing, but the rate on cards per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:27.480
mana just isn’t great on either of these.&nbsp;
Kwain and Selvala likewise get cut because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:32.840
like being able to choose which opponents get fed&nbsp;
cards—if one of my opponents is ahead, I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:38.280
be able to avoid giving them extra resources&nbsp;
while still being able to utilize my cards.

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:42.280
Another type of card I choose to cut is cards&nbsp;
that cost too much mana, since I’m trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:48.360
drop the mana curve. Octomancer is a funny card,&nbsp;
but this isn’t really a token deck and five mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:54.200
is just too much to pay for some vanilla stats.&nbsp;
Another cut is Coveted Jewel—it’s a funny card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:57.680
but this deck doesn’t have enough ways&nbsp;
of preventing opponents from snagging it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:03.360
and when it does get taken they don’t even need&nbsp;
to pay that expensive 6 mana up front like I did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:08.760
Most other cuts I made were cards that just&nbsp;
don’t do enough. Bloodroot Apothecary, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:15.240
it’s pre-selling for upwards of $10, seems highly&nbsp;
mediocre to me as anti-treasure tech—opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:19.640
can still afford to crack a few treasures at a&nbsp;
table without other poison, and then the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:24.400
can be saved for after the apothecary is off the&nbsp;
board. I’ll take a Manglehorn over this any day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:30.760
of the week. Now, a cut that truly pains me is&nbsp;
Jolly Gerbils—this card is extremely cute, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:36.080
the deck simply doesn’t have enough gift cards to&nbsp;
justify its inclusion, and even if I added a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:41.920
more I’m still not convinced it’d be preferable&nbsp;
to a better two drop. Another cut is Hoofprints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:47.560
of the Stag—this is a decent incremental value&nbsp;
piece if it’s played early, but it’s a very dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:53.280
draw later in the game. Triskaedekaphile is&nbsp;
another card I opted to cut—this deck wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:58.080
some number of no maximum hand size cards, but&nbsp;
the deck already has several other ones and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:03.240
card just doesn’t offer enough outside of&nbsp;
that to warrant keeping it in my opinion.

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:07.440
What goes in in place of these cards?&nbsp;
Beyond the replacements I already mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:13.120
a big area is +1/+1 counter synergies. A&nbsp;
lot of these are reasonably basic—Conclave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:18.640
Mentor is a bear that amplifies counters, Dusk&nbsp;
Legion Duelist is a bear that’s a draw engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:23.560
Benthic Biomancer is a similar card for one mana&nbsp;
that loots instead, and Kami of the Whispered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:29.520
Hopes is just a fantastic card, well worth the&nbsp;
$2 or so price tag. A couple others I picked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:34.320
out that likewise offer a bit of ramp&nbsp;
were Incubation Druid and Freestrider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:38.560
Lookout, both solid ramp options. Importantly,&nbsp;
I tried to pick out stuff that’s good in general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:43.680
and relatively low-cost. The issue with picking&nbsp;
out big, fancy payoff cards is that opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:47.920
can just deny your commander with removal, and&nbsp;
then suddenly you invested heavily in something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:52.680
that can’t actually materialize. Kami and&nbsp;
Druid in particular are especially great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:57.080
because without the commander they’re still&nbsp;
mana dorks, but with even a single targeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:01.600
with Ms. Bumbleflower’s ability either of&nbsp;
them can suddenly tap for three whole mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:07.360
and Kami only goes up from there. There are also&nbsp;
a few more specific cards from Modern Horizons 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:13.720
that I added, as that was a fantastic set for&nbsp;
+1/+1 counter synergies. Dreamtide Whale is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:19.240
card that feels like it was made for this deck—it&nbsp;
proliferates +1/+1 counters, it has a high power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:24.800
for delivering flying beats, and this is a deck&nbsp;
that wants to double-spell anyway so the whale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:29.840
should be sticking around. Evolution Witness is&nbsp;
another pretty sweet card—it’s not going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:34.240
wildly powerful in the early game, but later&nbsp;
in the game it can be an unstoppable torrent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:40.280
of value. The third card from this set I added is&nbsp;
Envoy of the Ancestors, and this is my answer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:45.320
the question of defensive cards and life total&nbsp;
races—just gain some life! This card sets up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:49.560
player for a truly impressive quantity of&nbsp;
lifegain, which will help when facing off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:53.760
against the final player and needing to survive&nbsp;
long enough to get the necessary attacks in.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:13:00.480
Beyond these, I also tweaked the interaction suite&nbsp;
a bit. I added Negate as a nice general-purpose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:05.160
counterspell to help with avoiding board wipes,&nbsp;
and I also swapped out Perplexing Test for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:10.320
slightly weirder five mana instant—Brokers&nbsp;
Confluence. This is a fun package for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:15.200
deck—it can shut off a problematic ability, it&nbsp;
can protect our board without losing the counters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:20.000
and it can also serve as a sort of overrun-type&nbsp;
card if we’re going in for the kill—you can always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:24.520
just Proliferate Proliferate Proliferate. Finally,&nbsp;
I also tweaked the manabase to have more green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:29.480
mana, since I just cut a bunch of blue and white&nbsp;
cards and added a bunch of green ones. Overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:33.880
this precon was quite solid, and I actually&nbsp;
considered changing fewer than my usual 20 cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:39.120
for this deck considering that. You could probably&nbsp;
drop the number of changes to 10 and still have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:43.600
a quite decent +1/+1 counter-based upgraded&nbsp;
precon, but I just felt like taking it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:49.440
the way with my changes. I didn’t talk about this&nbsp;
earlier beyond gesturing at the fact that tables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:54.480
will often have a player or two lagging behind,&nbsp;
but I imagine the sort of selective hug effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.480 --> 00:14:00.040
seen on Ms. Bumbleflower is likely an enjoyable&nbsp;
addition for a lot of tables. My issue with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:05.320
symmetrical group hug is that, though it means&nbsp;
everybody’s deck gets to do something, it often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:10.480
also means that the player who was getting ahead&nbsp;
anyway gets to have even more advantage. This more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:15.560
selective method of resource-giving, on the other&nbsp;
hand, can theoretically lead to more dynamic games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:20.800
with different players on a more equal footing.&nbsp;
To anybody who has played with this precon or this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:25.880
commander in general, let me know if this was&nbsp;
your experience, because I’m kind of curious.

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:28.800
Thanks for watching! If you like what&nbsp;
I make here and want some more of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:32.440
you can support me over on Patreon.&nbsp;
Recently I got a new camera lens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:37.520
and I’ve started recording less-scripted facecam&nbsp;
videos for ideas and thoughts that I’ve had but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:40.880
that haven’t quite made their way into an actual&nbsp;
video on the channel, and posting said videos on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:44.520
Patreon. One that I released a day or so&nbsp;
ago included random thoughts about some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:49.600
various Bloomburrow cards. If that sounds&nbsp;
up your alley, link is in the description.

